WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
Have you heard about School Incorporated?

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.040
It’s a new PBS series that

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:06.040
looks at America’s schools and

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:07.760
schools around the world.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.620
Makes interesting points about what’s

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:11.020
wrong with our schools

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:13.380
and what might be done about that.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:16.440
The series though is long, it’s 3 hours, so

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:19.860
here’s my Stossel short attention span version.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:22.040
Recognize this?

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:25.640
It’s the original Sony Walkman, introduced in 1979,

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:27.980
the first mainstream personal music player.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:29.360
That was just the beginning.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:32.420
Every year or two,

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:34.480
new and improved models hit the market.

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:38.080
To earn enough to buy the original Walkman,

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:40.080
you had to work two weeks

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:41.480
at a typical minimum wage job.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.020
And that was for lo-fi sound

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:46.860
on cassettes you had to flip over every half an hour.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:53.080
What’s really amazing about the rapid spread

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:55.300
and improvement of personal audio players

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:56.999
is that it isn’t amazing at all.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.420
It’s perfectly normal.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:01.220
A decade ago, no one had ever heard of Google.

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:03.160
Now they do tens of thousands

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:05.160
of internet searches per second.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:06.680
Facebook went from

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:10.400
zero to half-a-billion members in just 5 years.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.360
Basically, invent something good,

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.240
and it gets big.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.740
And these days, it gets big fast.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:18.120
But of all the products we make

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.980
and the services we provide,

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:21.140
there’s one that stands out

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:23.560
as an exception to that overall pattern;

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:26.160
one activity in which excellence doesn’t spawn

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:30.000
countless imitators or spread on a massive scale.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.540
And that exception

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:33.540
is schooling.

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:36.280
For generations,

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.840
there hasn’t been a single innovation in teaching

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:40.080
that has transformed

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.820
classrooms and improved student achievement

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:43.820
worldwide.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:45.140
Why not?

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:48.100
In America, the education establishment always says,

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:51.140
‘We need more money to makes schools better!'

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:53.360
But America's been trying that.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:56.260
We tripled spending on government run schools

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:58.140
over the past 50 years,

00:01:58.140 --> 00:01:58.880
and yet…

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.180
American test scores at the end of high school

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:04.140
have been flat since we started keeping track of them

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:06.580
all the way back in the&nbsp;early 1970's.

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:10.420
Basically, educational quality has been stuck

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:13.720
in the era of disco and leisure suits

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:16.280
And while our top athletes and pop stars reach

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:17.860
huge audiences,

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:20.760
our greatest teachers seldom reach more than

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:23.000
a few dozen kids at a time.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:24.780
Why not?

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:27.220
That’s the question at the heart of this series:

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:29.400
Coulson found some answers as to

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:32.040
why the best teachers don’t teach more kids

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.120
at California’s Garfield High.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:35.400
By 1988,

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.780
more students were passing the advanced placement

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:39.820
calculus test here at Garfield

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:41.760
than at Beverly Hills High.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:44.780
1 out of every 4 Mexican Americans who passed

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:47.000
AP calculus, nationwide,

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.880
attended Garfield.

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:50.200
Why?

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:51.400
Jaime Escalante

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:52.680
Jaime Escalante

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.620
Jaime Escalante

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:55.390
Time?

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:56.380
Three seconds

00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:58.120
As a teacher, he was fantastic.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:02.040
From the time he started teaching at Garfield in 1974,

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:03.780
Jaime Escalante worked as if his

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:06.660
life depended on the success of his students.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:08.620
There was nothing but excellence

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:10.380
expected of us students.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.240
We did not want to disappoint Jaime.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:15.400
By 1982, the results were beyond belief

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:16.280
Literally!

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:18.680
His students performed so far above expectations

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:20.200
on the AP calculus test

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:21.960
that the Educational Testing Service

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:24.400
suspected cheating and threw out their scores.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:26.240
Undaunted, they re-took it

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:28.840
and came through with flying colors a second time.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:31.200
Hollywood noticed, dramatizing the story in

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.280
the movie Stand and Deliver.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:35.540
This is basic math

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:37.980
but basic math is too easy for you, burros.

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:39.500
Seeing the movie makes me laugh,

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:40.860
it gives me a lot of memories as to

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:42.280
how it was in the classroom.

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:43.680
He taught me everything I needed

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.620
to be prepared for college.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:46.860
In art as in life,

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:49.760
Escalante had a simple message for his students:

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:52.060
with enough drive and hard work,

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:53.520
the sky was the limit.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:55.280
When his students went to college,

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:57.480
they found they were extra-well prepared.

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:00.040
Every one of those classes that I took

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:02.700
there was always a subject that I had already learned

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:05.540
in Mr. Jaime Escalante’s class in high school.

00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:06.550
Louder!

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:09.680
A negative times a negative equals a positive.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:11.140
Why?

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:13.640
The movie Stand and Deliver ends on a high note

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:15.600
with Escalante’s students proving the

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:16.760
skeptics wrong.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:20.560
But the story of his mathematics program at Garfield

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.560
does not have a Hollywood ending.

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:24.380
Every single week

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:25.920
there was a new

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:27.820
film crew coming in.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:28.880
In any other field,

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:31.620
we might expect this combination of success,

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:33.400
scalability, and publicity to

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:36.340
have catapulted Escalante to the top of his profession.

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:39.280
Or like Hubble’s expanding universe,

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:42.180
to have spread all across the country.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:43.340
That just isn’t what happened.

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:45.900
A  lot of the teachers were resentful,

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:47.740
and it was very public.

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:49.800
The key

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:52.620
problem was that Escalante's classes were big.

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:53.920
He could handle it.

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:55.500
He was setting a precedent.

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:57.520
He was giving the message to the administrator:

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:59.560
“If Escalante can do it, why not you?”

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:01.960
So the teachers union marshaled enough votes

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:05.100
to oust Escalante as head of the math department.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:07.980
Demoted and resented by many of his colleagues,

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:10.040
Escalante left Garfield High.

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:12.780
That’s the bad news.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:14.180
The good news is that

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:15.320
there are places

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:17.960
where educational excellence is scaling up,

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:21.340
And so Coulson went to some of those places.

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:24.380
Sweden, Chile, London, India, Korea.

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:25.600
In Korea...

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:27.680
The top teachers earn more

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:30.200
than the highest paid professional baseball players.

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:34.660
My whole lecture revenue

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:35.980
is over

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:37.980
100 million dollars.

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:39.620
Really,

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:42.300
100 million dollars!

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:45.220
Coulson and I will explain how that happens

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:47.000
in the next video.

